'''vulgarization''' (vul″gär-i-zā′shọn), ''n.'' [< ''vulgarize'' + ''-ation.''] '''1.''' Wide dissemination; the process of rendering commonly known or familiar.

{{smaller block|The inclusion of anthropology in the general exhibition of liberal arts is of great value in respect of that ''vulgarization'' which is the aim of the French anthropologists.
{{right|''Athenæum'', No. 3225, p. 229.}}

Within the last few years competent authorities of different countries have been preoccupied with the inconveniences and injury that may result to public health and morality by the ''vulgarisation'' of hypnotic phenomena.
{{right|''Lancet'', 1889, I. 861.}}}}
'''2.''' A making coarse or gross; the impairing of refinement or elegance.

{{smaller block|Persia has thus fairly well escaped ''vulgarization'' and misrepresentation at the hands of the globe-trotter, with his worthless “impressions.”
{{right|''Westminster Rev.'', CXXVIII. 454.}}}}
Also spelled ''vulgarisation.''

'''vulgarize''' (vul′gär-īz), ''v.''; pret. and pp. ''vulgarized'', ppr. ''vulgarizing.'' [< F. ''vulgariser'' = Sp. Pg. ''vulgarizar'' = It. ''vulgarizzare''; as ''vulgar'' + ''-ize.''] '''I.''' ''trans.'' To make vulgar or common.

{{smaller block|The care of Augustus Cæsar, ne nomen suum obsolefieret, that the majesty of his name should not be ''vulgarized'' by bad poets, is more seriously needed in our days on behalf of great poets, to protect them from trivial or too parrot-like a citation.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Style, iii.}}

His marriage to that woman has hopelessly ''vulgarized'' him.
{{right|''Thackeray'', Vanity Fair, xxv.}}

The image is, therefore, out of all imaginative keeping, and ''vulgarizes'' the chief personage in a grand historical tragedy, who, if not a great, was at least a decorous actor.
{{right|''Lowell'', Among my Books, 2d ser., p. 262.}}}}
'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To produce vulgarity.

